A||||8||artherd||Dry Sump||||3||
Z||000000||artherd||09-27-2001||03:31 AM||||Hrm, I was looking through a Summit catalouge with a friend today, and got to thinking...<P>They sell dry-sump multistage "scavange/pressure" pumps...<P>What are the caveates, if any, of converting an wet-sump engine to dry system?  (my application is a northstar, so won't require a cam/lifter valley scavanger at all.  Just block and 2 heads.)<P>Thoughts?<P>Best!!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000001||Nashco||09-27-2001||04:20 AM||||Hmm...very interesting thoughts entering my head.  I'll bite my tongue for a while and do some research while seeing what others have to say about this <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||EdsB52||09-27-2001||04:23 AM||||What's the advantage in a car? In aircraft it enables a person to go upside down for sustained periods. Why in car?||||reg||1
Z||000003||ray b||09-27-2001||11:15 AM||||dry sumps = less drag from oil flying around in crankcase, better cooling, and less foaming of the oil. all lead to better oiling at more stable pressure and donot starve in hard turns [ do to oil being pushed away from pickups]<BR>it is not needed in a normal street car but is common race practis.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||09-27-2001||12:11 PM||||What Ray said but it really isn't needed unless you plan on some heavy duty (competition) racing for $$.  Disadvantage is belt driven critical component and more drag due to the multiple stages.  You will still get windage oil though because the oil will still drain down into the pan pickups, but the vortex won't pickup oil from a sump during lateral Gs in any direction.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 09-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||webbee||09-27-2001||01:06 PM||||This install adds a collection container to an already tight engine bay. It's not needed on a streeter.<P>Waste your money on suspension mods. You'll get more bang for your buck.||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||09-27-2001||08:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>What's the advantage in a car? In aircraft it enables a person to go upside down for sustained periods. Why in car?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Similar reasons, prevents (delays and absorbs actually) oil starvation due to g-loading. (usually dry sump pans have multiple scavangeing pickups, often feeding seperate pump stages even, so even durning cornering, you not only have a full 6-odd quart resivor, but are still scavanging from at least one pickup.)<P>Also, you have the option of useing a lower oil pan that need only barely clear the crank (setting the engine's CG lower in the car.)<P>Usually liberates some horsepower from removing the crank from it's foamy oil bath too. (despite a much more 'massive' oil pump  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> (about 5% of output, or around 20bhp on a 400bhp v8 engine, give or take.)<P>Ferrari roadgoing V8s have been dry-sump since the 348 (the 360 has a cool prominent resivor  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I think I will drive the oil pump electronically, to avoid higher RPM losses.<P>At this point, I'm not sure I'll be able to finance it, but if not, then the next Northstar car will definately be dry-sumping  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>It's basically a racecar thing, but I may be able to put it together for only about $400.<P>This car is being designed to see some track time too. And I don't mean just autocrosses...<P>There's a lot left to do still, I think I may end up building the car in two 'stages'. Engine first, then upgrade the drivetrain and suspension and brakes and wheels/tyres in stage 2 (they essentially all have to be done as a set the way I want to do them.  a&gt; requires b&gt; which needs d&gt; e&gt; and f&gt; and such.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000007||Leper||09-27-2001||08:51 PM||||Another advantage is that you can mount the engine lower in the engine compartmen due to the smaller oil pan. I don't know it that would work in a fiero though.<BR>Porsche uses a dry sump system that holds about 12 quarts of oil. Get rid of the spare tire and you can stick an oil reservoir there and relocate some more weight to the front of the car and possibly lower the center of gravity some.||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||09-27-2001||10:42 PM||||The oil reciever made by Accusump will work too.<BR>Less $||||reg||1
